      <h2>2. How People Use Video Chat</h2>

      <p>Video calls are useful in situations where seeing and hearing another person adds value to the interaction. The best format depends on the purpose of the conversation.</p>

      <h3>2.1 Friends and Family</h3>

      <p>Video calls can help friends and family communicate across distance. Seeing another person's expressions and surroundings can make a remote conversation feel more interactive than exchanging short text messages.</p>

      <h3>2.2 Language Practice</h3>

      <p>Video conversations give language learners an opportunity to practice listening and speaking in real time. They can also expose learners to pronunciation, conversational pacing and informal expressions.</p>

      <p>However, a casual conversation should complement rather than replace structured language instruction when a learner needs systematic grammar or vocabulary development.</p>

      <h3>2.3 Remote Work and Collaboration</h3>

      <p>Teams may use video calls for meetings, interviews, presentations, design reviews and discussions that benefit from immediate feedback.</p>

      <p>Not every task requires video, however. A short message can sometimes be more efficient when the information does not require real-time discussion.</p>

      <h3>2.4 Meeting New People</h3>

      <p>Random video chat creates a different experience because the participants may not know each other beforehand. This can provide opportunities for spontaneous conversation, cultural exchange and discovering shared interests.</p>

