      <h2>3. How Online Video Chat Works Technically</h2>

      <p>A video call looks simple to a user, but several technologies work together behind the scenes. The browser needs to obtain permission to use the camera and microphone, establish a connection with the other participant, negotiate media settings, and continuously adapt to changing network conditions.</p>

      <h3>3.1 WebRTC</h3>

      <p>WebRTC is a set of web technologies that enables browsers to exchange real-time audio, video and data. It is widely used for browser-based communication.</p>

      <h3>3.2 Signaling</h3>

      <p>Before two browsers can communicate, they need to exchange information about the proposed connection. This process is commonly called signaling. A signaling server can help participants exchange session information and network candidates, but it does not necessarily carry the actual video stream.</p>

      <h3>3.3 STUN and TURN</h3>

      <p>Direct browser-to-browser connections are not always possible because devices may be behind routers, firewalls or network address translation. WebRTC uses ICE to find a workable connection path.</p>

      <p>STUN can help a device discover the network address it can use for communication. If a direct connection cannot be established, TURN can relay traffic through a server.</p>

      <h3>3.4 Adaptive Quality</h3>

      <p>Network conditions can change during a call. A video system may adjust resolution, bitrate or other media parameters to keep the conversation usable when bandwidth or connection quality changes.</p>

      <p>In many situations, maintaining understandable audio is more important than maintaining the highest possible video resolution.</p>

