      <h3>10.4 Choosing Whether to Continue a Conversation</h3>

      <p>Random conversation works best when both participants want to continue. If a conversation is uncomfortable or inappropriate, leaving is always an option.</p>

      <h2>Conclusion: Choosing the Right Kind of Video Conversation</h2>

      <p>Online video chat has become a practical communication format for friends, families, coworkers, learners and people who want to meet someone new. Its main difference from text is the combination of real-time audio and visual communication.</p>

      <p>Video is not automatically better than text or voice. Each format has different strengths. Text is efficient for asynchronous communication, voice provides real-time conversation without a camera, and video adds visual information when that information is useful.</p>

      <p>When talking with strangers, the benefits of spontaneity should be balanced with privacy and safety awareness. Avoid sharing sensitive information, be cautious about requests for money, use available reporting and blocking tools, and leave conversations that make you uncomfortable.</p>

      <p>If you want to try a spontaneous video conversation, <strong><a href="/">Wakiee</a></strong> is one option. Before using any online communication service, including Wakiee, review its current privacy information and community guidelines so you understand how the service works.</p>

